Tobemstomig —F. Hoffmann -La Roche Ltd  
156/Protocol BO44178 , Version 4 ENDOCRINE EVENTS  
Management guidelines for endocrine events are provided in  Table  A6-4. 
Participants  with unexplained symptoms , such as headache, fatigue, myalgias, 
impotence, constipation, or mental sta tus changes , should be investigated for the 
presence of thyroid, pituitary, or adrenal endocrinopathies.  The participant should be 
referred to an endocrinologist if an endocrinopathy is suspected.  Thyroid -stimulating 
hormone (TSH) and free triiodothyroni ne and thyroxine levels should be measured to 
determine whether thyroid abnormalities are present.  Pituitary hormone levels and 
function tests (e.g.,  TSH, growth hormone, luteinizing hormone, follicle -stimulating 
hormone, testosterone, prolactin, adrenoco rticotropic hormone [ACTH] levels, and ACTH 
stimulation test) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain (with detailed 
pituitary sections) may help to differentiate primary pituitary insufficiency from primary 
adrenal insufficiency.  
  

